3
00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:09.820
but as you'll see, it's very straightforward

4
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.660
if we're layering up these approaches

5
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.580
and learning about each step as we go.

6
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.699
So we'll cover what material functions are,

7
00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:18.820
we'll talk about using function inputs,

8
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.699
we'll talk about building an animated material function,

9
00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:23.739
and then we'll also use that material function

10
00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:25.820
in some form of master material.

11
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.480
So what is a material function?

12
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.640
So material function allows you to embed materials

13
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.960
within materials, avoid an unnecessary clutter.

14
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.000
It also just allows for reuse in various

15
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.560
either projects, packages, multiple materials.

16
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.679
It's kind of reusing that functionality

17
00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:47.780
so you don't have to keep creating

18
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.820
or coding the same thing over and over again.

19
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.920
So if you get a really nice, simple grass wind shader,

20
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.380
for example, in this example,

21
00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:59.700
you've created a really nice water shader

22
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.060
that you really want to reuse throughout your projects.

23
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.379
Material functions are a really nice, clean way of saying,

24
00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:08.420
here's some complex piece of information.

25
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.560
I mean, it can be lightweight,

26
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.620
but generally it'd be something a bit more complex

27
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.340
where you've created this functionality

28
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.420
and you can just use this as a function

29
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.500
to plug into an existing material

30
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.780
to get the desired results

31
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.879
so you don't have to keep reworking existing work.

32
00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:25.219
So we can use function inputs.

33
00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:27.659
So function inputs allow us to create

34
00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:29.620
some kind of customized data

35
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.959
and we can edit the sorting priority,

36
00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:34.939
which allows control on where the new node is

37
00:01:34.939 --> 00:01:37.260
and placed within the material function

38
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.540
and the material destination.

39
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.000
So how to create this is that we want to first create

40
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.299
a material function.

41
00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:46.019
And you can just do this by right clicking

42
00:01:46.019 --> 00:01:47.340
in your content browser,

43
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.560
generally where you'd store other materials.

44
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.879
Maybe you'd create a material function folder specifically

45
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:55.120
so you know where all your material functions are.

46
00:01:55.879 --> 00:01:58.640
Again, it's just dependent on what your preferred workflow is.

47
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.439
Essentially, you just want to right click

48
00:02:00.439 --> 00:02:01.760
and go to materials and textures

49
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.480
and create the material function from there.

50
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.400
Then you can open it up and create the system.

51
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.400
So this is an in-class exercise.

52
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.680
So we can go through this in the in-class portion.

53
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.919
However, even though there's a lot of nodes going on,

54
00:02:14.919 --> 00:02:17.000
it's definitely not as hard as it seems.

55
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.259
We provided samples and based on the previous example

56
00:02:21.259 --> 00:02:23.680
that we covered in the previous section,

57
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.560
you can see that it's a lot of copy and pasted information.

58
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.120
So we've set up one section and then we just duplicated it

59
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.960
and we're trying to dial in the desired results.

60
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.439
So always just break down the material

61
00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:38.000
to its component parts, kind of work backwards.

62
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.120
And you'll see that a lot of the information

63
00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.360
is then duplicated and replicated in different ways

64
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.780
to achieve this result.

65
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.659
And so once we've created that material function,

66
00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:50.800
we can actually just use that

67
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.560
in a different master material now.

68
00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:55.039
So we can select the main material node

69
00:02:55.039 --> 00:02:58.120
